; Mail routing (RFC 5321 §5.1, RFC 7505, RFC 2181 §10.3).
;
; The same records are correct or broken depending on what the domain is for. A
; null MX on a sending-only domain is deliberate and right; the same record on a
; domain that expects mail means every message bounces. Both are here, and the
; only difference between them is what the caller says the domain is for.
$ORIGIN mx.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.

; Two exchanges, both resolvable. Nothing wrong here.
@                   IN MX   10 mail1.mx.test.
@                   IN MX   20 mail2.mx.test.
mail1               IN A    198.51.100.80
mail2               IN A    198.51.100.81

; RFC 7505. The trailing dot is the root, and it is the whole record: this
; domain accepts no mail and says so.
nomail              IN MX   0 .

; A null MX alongside a real one. Section 3 forbids the pair, and senders
; disagree about what it means, so delivery depends on whose MTA is trying.
ambiguous           IN MX   0 .
ambiguous           IN MX   10 mail1.mx.test.

; The exchange exists as a name and has no address of either family.
dangling            IN MX   10 nowhere.mx.test.
nowhere             IN TXT  "a name with no address"

; RFC 2181 §10.3 forbids an MX pointing at an alias. Most senders follow it
; anyway, which is exactly why the ones that refuse look intermittent.
aliased             IN MX   10 alias.mx.test.
alias               IN CNAME mail1.mx.test.

; An address where a name belongs. The zone file accepts it, dig prints it, and
; senders look it up as a name and find nothing.
literal             IN MX   10 198.51.100.80.

; No MX at all, but an address record — so mail goes to whatever runs there.
implicit            IN A    198.51.100.82

; Neither MX nor address. Nothing can deliver here at all.
undeliverable       IN TXT  "no mx, no address"

; IPv6 only. Unusual and legitimate: the absence of an A record is not the
; absence of an address.
sixth               IN MX   10 mail6.mx.test.
mail6               IN AAAA 2001:db8:6a11::1
